WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.356 --> 00:00:01.333
- Good morning Believe Nation,

00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:03.196
it's Evan, my one word is believe.

00:00:03.196 --> 00:00:05.551
And I believe that you
have something special

00:00:05.551 --> 00:00:06.733
that when you unleash it,

00:00:06.733 --> 00:00:08.973
it will be able to change the world.

00:00:08.973 --> 00:00:10.163
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:12.570
today's message is seek mentors.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.234
Over to you, Robert Kiyosaki.

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:17.157
(rooster crowing)

00:00:17.157 --> 00:00:19.553
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:22.116
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:26.418
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:26.418 --> 00:00:28.020
- [Interview] If you did not
have the rich dad in your life,

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.359
what would you be doing today?

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:32.047
- It's hard to say if I
didn't have my rich dad,

00:00:32.047 --> 00:00:36.214
because as a young kid,
starting at probably the age

00:00:37.425 --> 00:00:39.878
of five, six, seven, eight, nine,

00:00:39.878 --> 00:00:41.793
I really wanted to be rich.

00:00:41.793 --> 00:00:45.145
So I think number one is desire.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:49.306
And then you naturally seek
your role models and teachers.

00:00:49.306 --> 00:00:52.416
And in my lifetime, I've
had multiple Rich Dads.

00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:54.411
You know my rich dad was
my best friend's father

00:00:54.411 --> 00:00:56.252
starting at the age of nine.

00:00:56.252 --> 00:00:58.508
And then today I still seek mentors,

00:00:58.508 --> 00:01:00.544
and wise men and wise women,

00:01:00.544 --> 00:01:03.477
because that's how you get wiser

00:01:03.477 --> 00:01:05.849
is by hanging out with smart people.

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:07.402
- I'm a big believer in mentorship.

00:01:07.402 --> 00:01:09.701
One, in terms of face to face people

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:11.123
that you can go and learn from,

00:01:11.123 --> 00:01:13.145
and two, from what I call
aspirational mentors,

00:01:13.145 --> 00:01:14.528
people who've done a lot more than you

00:01:14.528 --> 00:01:16.608
who you may never meet,
who may have passed on,

00:01:16.608 --> 00:01:18.274
but you can still learn
from their success.

00:01:18.274 --> 00:01:19.769
It comes from the core understanding

00:01:19.769 --> 00:01:22.536
that whatever challenge you
have in front of you right now

00:01:22.536 --> 00:01:25.317
somebody else has already solved it.

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:28.227
Whatever big problem
you're facing in your life

00:01:28.227 --> 00:01:29.307
or in your business,

00:01:29.307 --> 00:01:31.342
somebody has already solved it

00:01:31.342 --> 00:01:32.500
and you don't need to come up

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.777
with every ingenious solution yourself.

00:01:34.777 --> 00:01:37.207
This is one of the things
that saved my first business.

00:01:37.207 --> 00:01:39.029
One of the two most important lessons

00:01:39.029 --> 00:01:40.405
that I've ever learned in my life,

00:01:40.405 --> 00:01:42.540
was whenever I don't know what to do,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.228
whenever I have a big problem
and I don't have the answer,

00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:47.188
I look for somebody who
has already solved it

00:01:47.188 --> 00:01:48.841
and I try to learn from their lessons.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:50.833
In my first business I
had a software company,

00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:53.933
I was struggling, making $300 a month,

00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:55.171
barely able to survive,

00:01:55.171 --> 00:01:56.581
and I was just trying to come up

00:01:56.581 --> 00:01:58.027
with all these ideas myself.

00:01:58.027 --> 00:01:59.367
Like okay how 'about we do
this, how 'about you do this,

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.008
how 'about you, and nothing was working.

00:02:01.008 --> 00:02:02.740
And I told my partner I quit

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.812
and it was the worst day of my life.

00:02:04.812 --> 00:02:06.626
And when I woke up the next day I realized

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:07.661
I didn't want to quit,

00:02:07.661 --> 00:02:10.390
I just couldn't keep
going the way I was going.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.625
And the light bulb moment was I realized

00:02:12.625 --> 00:02:15.110
I'm not the first guy to
try to sell software before.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.988
Maybe our specific kind of software, yes,

00:02:17.988 --> 00:02:20.831
but in general, other
people have figured out

00:02:20.831 --> 00:02:23.027
how to sell software
and make lots of money,

00:02:23.027 --> 00:02:25.031
and target lots of customers.

00:02:25.031 --> 00:02:26.415
How did they do it?

00:02:26.415 --> 00:02:28.682
And I looked at Bill Gates
and how he got started

00:02:28.682 --> 00:02:30.114
and that started the process

00:02:30.114 --> 00:02:32.236
to me being able to build a real company.

00:02:32.236 --> 00:02:35.504
And that lesson that I
learned, at 19 years old,

00:02:35.504 --> 00:02:38.286
has stayed true for the
past, almost, 20 years,

00:02:38.286 --> 00:02:40.585
where every time I don't know what to do

00:02:40.585 --> 00:02:42.026
I look at who can I model.

00:02:42.026 --> 00:02:43.324
It's why the name of this channel

00:02:43.324 --> 00:02:44.830
is Modeling The Masters.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.235
It's why I try to showcase

00:02:46.235 --> 00:02:48.225
lots of different successful people

00:02:48.225 --> 00:02:50.937
from all walks of life because
you can learn from them,

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:52.484
I'm learning from them.

00:02:52.484 --> 00:02:55.682
And even today, in all
my different businesses

00:02:55.682 --> 00:02:59.012
earlier this week, I went
to SoulCycle with Alex,

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.450
and SoulCycle's a spinning company

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.466
that has a great culture,
recently came to Toronto,

00:03:03.466 --> 00:03:05.873
I think they're based in
New York, might be LA.

00:03:05.873 --> 00:03:08.488
But they came to Toronto,
they opened up a location,

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.527
and I heard so much about their culture

00:03:10.527 --> 00:03:13.107
that I thought I'm trying to
build a culture here at TDS,

00:03:13.107 --> 00:03:15.593
we have what I think is
a really good culture,

00:03:15.593 --> 00:03:17.152
if you can compare our culture

00:03:17.152 --> 00:03:20.016
to most businesses I think it's amazing.

00:03:20.016 --> 00:03:22.171
But it could always be better, right?

00:03:22.171 --> 00:03:24.191
I want to model even greater success.

00:03:24.191 --> 00:03:26.306
And so Alex and I went to the class.

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:27.495
And we learned from them.

00:03:27.495 --> 00:03:29.655
We saw how they set up their store,

00:03:29.655 --> 00:03:32.363
and their merch, and how
the staff greets you,

00:03:32.363 --> 00:03:34.080
and how the instructor starts a class,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.616
and runs a class, and ends a class,

00:03:35.616 --> 00:03:37.591
and what happens in the locker room.

00:03:37.591 --> 00:03:39.928
All the different pieces
you learn from them.

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:41.698
It doesn't mean you're
going to apply everything,

00:03:41.698 --> 00:03:44.760
but if you take one lesson
here, and one lesson here,

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:45.758
and one lesson here,

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:49.243
from people and companies
that have had great success

00:03:49.243 --> 00:03:51.451
it just makes you better.

00:03:51.451 --> 00:03:54.038
And so I would seek out mentors

00:03:54.038 --> 00:03:55.481
that you can meet face to face

00:03:55.481 --> 00:03:58.013
or you can do now Google Hangouts with,

00:03:58.013 --> 00:03:59.157
or ask questions from.

00:03:59.157 --> 00:04:00.556
If anybody's doing a livestream,

00:04:00.556 --> 00:04:02.087
you get a chance to ask a question.

00:04:02.087 --> 00:04:04.424
I wish that existed when
I was getting started

00:04:04.424 --> 00:04:05.541
as an entrepreneur.

00:04:05.541 --> 00:04:08.000
You have so much more access to people

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.673
than we've ever had before

00:04:09.673 --> 00:04:11.892
but even if you're not
going to the direct approach

00:04:11.892 --> 00:04:12.962
and hitting somebody,

00:04:12.962 --> 00:04:15.476
look into the aspirational mentors.

00:04:15.476 --> 00:04:17.617
Find people who've done it before,

00:04:17.617 --> 00:04:18.756
even if they've passed on,

00:04:18.756 --> 00:04:21.690
their legacy have left a series of clues

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.247
that if you can follow those clues

00:04:23.247 --> 00:04:25.497
you can apply it to your
life, to your business,

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:26.631
and to success.

00:04:26.631 --> 00:04:28.541
Whenever you don't know what to do,

00:04:28.541 --> 00:04:31.255
instead of trying to
figure it all out yourself,

00:04:31.255 --> 00:04:33.807
understand people have done it before,

00:04:33.807 --> 00:04:36.170
seek them out, learn from them,

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:39.779
apply it to your business, and
you can have success as well.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:40.751
So the question of today is

00:04:40.751 --> 00:04:43.862
I'm curious who has been
your biggest mentor,

00:04:43.862 --> 00:04:46.299
either in your personal life
or in your business career,

00:04:46.299 --> 00:04:48.815
who has been the biggest
mentor for you and why.

00:04:48.815 --> 00:04:49.918
I'd love to hear from you,

00:04:49.918 --> 00:04:52.169
please share your thoughts
down in the comments below.

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:53.282
I also want to give a quick shoutout

00:04:53.282 --> 00:04:55.902
to Roberto Latini,
Roberto thank you so much

00:04:55.902 --> 00:04:58.079
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word.

00:04:58.079 --> 00:04:59.732
I really, really appreciate the support

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.337
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:05:01.337 --> 00:05:02.851
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:05:02.851 --> 00:05:03.877
I believe in you,

00:05:03.877 --> 00:05:05.699
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:07.519
and whatever your one word is.

00:05:07.519 --> 00:05:10.615
Much love, and I'll see you
guys again tomorrow morning

00:05:10.615 --> 00:05:13.404
for another shot of entspresso.

00:05:13.404 --> 00:05:15.611
♫ I wake up every morning

00:05:15.611 --> 00:05:19.792
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:05:19.792 --> 00:05:22.414
(typing)

00:05:22.414 --> 00:05:24.594
- Where you had an early
meeting with Dino De Laurentiis

00:05:24.594 --> 00:05:25.852
that didn't go so well.

00:05:25.852 --> 00:05:28.642
- Well, that was different
because Dino De Laurentiis

00:05:28.642 --> 00:05:32.075
had an office, he wanted
me to be in Dark Savage.

00:05:32.075 --> 00:05:34.716
And he wanted me to play
the villain in Dark Savage.

00:05:34.716 --> 00:05:38.383
And I walked in, and I
always kind of have a

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:44.099
mouth that sometimes goes
a little bit too fast.

00:05:44.099 --> 00:05:45.849
And I step on myself.

00:05:47.298 --> 00:05:48.716
I walked into the office

00:05:48.716 --> 00:05:51.860
and I saw Dino standing behind his desk.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.102
And it was this huge desk,

00:05:54.102 --> 00:05:57.604
ornate and gold, kind of French
looking or Italian looking,

00:05:57.604 --> 00:06:00.329
I don't know what, but it was gigantic.

00:06:00.329 --> 00:06:02.537
It was one of those popular desks, right.

00:06:02.537 --> 00:06:04.375
And Dino, the only thing that stood out

00:06:04.375 --> 00:06:06.634
was kind of his chest and his head

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:08.955
because he's a little guy,
Dino's a really short guy.

00:06:08.955 --> 00:06:12.094
So I looked in, I walked in his office,

00:06:12.094 --> 00:06:14.000
and I looked and I says hi Dino,

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.828
why does a little guy like
you need such a big desk?

00:06:17.828 --> 00:06:20.478
(audience laughing)

00:06:20.478 --> 00:06:24.455
And to Dino De Laurentiis you
never say you're little, right

00:06:24.455 --> 00:06:27.061
because he saw himself as a giant.

00:06:27.061 --> 00:06:30.831
So he looked at me and he
says, ah Schwarzenegger,

00:06:30.831 --> 00:06:35.028
you have an accent, I can not use you, ah.

00:06:35.028 --> 00:06:36.808
(audience laughing)

00:06:36.808 --> 00:06:39.058
And I said, again stupidly,

00:06:40.917 --> 00:06:42.526
who is talking about having an accent.

00:06:42.526 --> 00:06:43.359
Like Dino?

00:06:45.588 --> 00:06:48.894
Ah, are you a Nazi, ah, you know.

00:06:48.894 --> 00:06:51.996
And he walked away because
I had a German accent.

00:06:51.996 --> 00:06:53.501
So that was the end of that.

00:06:53.501 --> 00:06:57.116
So I left the office and
my agent came up to me,

00:06:57.116 --> 00:07:00.023
grabbed me, really was for my arm,

00:07:00.023 --> 00:07:04.355
and he said to me, he says I've
been an agent for 15 years,

00:07:04.355 --> 00:07:08.281
this meeting was exactly
one minute and 14 seconds,

00:07:08.281 --> 00:07:10.209
it was the shortest
meeting we've ever had,

00:07:10.209 --> 00:07:12.762
you just everything up, God damn it,

00:07:12.762 --> 00:07:14.684
it took me months to get in here.

00:07:14.684 --> 00:07:16.069
So he was mad at me also.

00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:19.479
So anyways, so that's how
the relationship started

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:20.535
with Dino.

00:07:20.535 --> 00:07:23.444
But then when he bought Conan,

00:07:23.444 --> 00:07:26.064
he came to Spain where we were shooting

00:07:26.064 --> 00:07:27.838
and the third day of filming,

00:07:27.838 --> 00:07:31.166
after he saw the day list, the
footage that had been filmed,

00:07:31.166 --> 00:07:35.033
he came up to me and
he said Schwarzenegger

00:07:35.033 --> 00:07:36.533
you are Conan, ah.

00:07:37.483 --> 00:07:38.826
And he walked out.

00:07:38.826 --> 00:07:41.401
And Milius came to me,
the director, and says

00:07:41.401 --> 00:07:43.710
this is the greatest compliment
that he can ever get.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.626
He said you're Conan, isn't that great.

00:07:45.626 --> 00:07:46.987
I said I guess so.

00:07:46.987 --> 00:07:48.847
And from that point on we created

00:07:48.847 --> 00:07:50.398
this really great relationship

00:07:50.398 --> 00:07:52.221
and we did movies together.

00:07:52.221 --> 00:07:54.775
And the Raw Deal, and then Red Sonja,

00:07:54.775 --> 00:07:57.498
and Conan The Destroyer,
and Conan The Barbarian.

00:07:57.498 --> 00:08:01.453
And he was became kind
of like a father figure.

00:08:01.453 --> 00:08:03.305
He became kind of the Reg Park

00:08:03.305 --> 00:08:07.324
that I had in the bodybuilding
world, and Jowita,

00:08:07.324 --> 00:08:10.250
he became that character
in the movie world.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:11.843
That I could go to for anything

00:08:11.843 --> 00:08:13.436
who will give me advice,

00:08:13.436 --> 00:08:15.646
who always said wonderful compliments,

00:08:15.646 --> 00:08:19.777
and kind of took me in
as part of his family,

00:08:19.777 --> 00:08:21.928
and so that's why I always
will miss him very dearly

00:08:21.928 --> 00:08:23.601
after he passed away.

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:27.412
- How do we find our
personal Kurt Kent Meyers?

00:08:27.412 --> 00:08:29.999
Somebody who can really
speak into our lives

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:32.418
and change our trajectory.

00:08:32.418 --> 00:08:34.628
- Well I can't take credit for it.

00:08:34.628 --> 00:08:39.300
I went to seminar that Kurt
was teaching on success

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.361
and when he finished
teaching, I went up to him

00:08:42.361 --> 00:08:44.206
and introduced myself,

00:08:44.206 --> 00:08:47.224
and I asked for an appointment with him.

00:08:47.224 --> 00:08:49.415
And he came then to Lancaster,

00:08:49.415 --> 00:08:50.973
to the Holiday Inn for breakfast,

00:08:50.973 --> 00:08:53.328
and we had that breakfast
meeting, that did change my life.

00:08:53.328 --> 00:08:55.372
And then I never saw
him again from that time

00:08:55.372 --> 00:08:57.754
until my 65th birthday

00:08:57.754 --> 00:08:58.867
when my staff brought,

00:08:58.867 --> 00:09:00.881
I never had seen him, I talked about him,

00:09:00.881 --> 00:09:01.737
I've talked about,

00:09:01.737 --> 00:09:03.065
and on my 65th birthday,

00:09:03.065 --> 00:09:04.974
when we were having a party together,

00:09:04.974 --> 00:09:07.271
they brought him into the room.

00:09:07.271 --> 00:09:08.804
And I hadn't seen him for 40 years.

00:09:08.804 --> 00:09:10.273
I just wept and I thought, you know.

00:09:10.273 --> 00:09:13.202
But I think the key was
when I went to hear him,

00:09:13.202 --> 00:09:14.954
and he began to speak to my life

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:16.379
about being successful,

00:09:16.379 --> 00:09:19.395
I kind of broke out of a barrier

00:09:19.395 --> 00:09:20.599
and I went up to introduce myself

00:09:20.599 --> 00:09:22.849
and then I asked could
I take him to breakfast

00:09:22.849 --> 00:09:24.127
and could I learn from him.

00:09:24.127 --> 00:09:26.346
And I think they key is
once you see somebody

00:09:26.346 --> 00:09:30.234
that is helping you, be bold, and ask them

00:09:30.234 --> 00:09:31.879
can I take you to breakfast.

00:09:31.879 --> 00:09:35.196
And by the way here's a thought,
buy the breakfast, okay.

00:09:35.196 --> 00:09:38.608
Just don't go and say hey how
'about buying breakfast for me.

00:09:38.608 --> 00:09:42.984
I took that step, and I've
always been glad I took that step

00:09:42.984 --> 00:09:44.049
and I've always thought to myself

00:09:44.049 --> 00:09:47.002
you know what if I wouldn't
have taken the step,

00:09:47.002 --> 00:09:50.231
then I probably would have
never had that relationship,

00:09:50.231 --> 00:09:51.931
never maybe would have
asked that question,

00:09:51.931 --> 00:09:53.841
never would have had this seminar,

00:09:53.841 --> 00:09:56.148
or would have written the book.

00:09:56.148 --> 00:09:57.927
So I think once you see somebody

00:09:57.927 --> 00:09:59.575
that you think can really help you,

00:09:59.575 --> 00:10:01.416
take the initiative and step right up.

